Credit cards are payment instruments issued by financial institutions that allow consumers and businesses to purchase goods and services using an approved line of credit. Cardholders repay the borrowed amount according to the issuer's terms, with interest and fees potentially applying depending on the balance and payment arrangements.

Credit cards remain an important part of the global financial services ecosystem, supporting retail transactions, travel bookings, entertainment spending, recurring payments, and e-commerce. Issuers compete through reward programs, credit limits, security features, customer service, and digital banking capabilities.

Asia-Pacific accounts for approximately 36% of global market consumption, supported by expanding financial inclusion, a growing middle class, and increased adoption of digital payment services.

